{{also|hang over}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|hang-over}}

===Etymology===
[[American English]]; {{af|en|hang|over}}. First sense was first attested in 1904. Second sense was first attested in 1894.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈhæŋoʊvɚ/|a=GenAm}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈhæŋəʊvə/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-hangover.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q207334}}Negative [[effect]]s, such as [[headache]] or [[nausea]], caused by previous [[drunkenness]] due to (excessive) consumption of [[alcohol]].
#: {{syn|en|veisalgia}}
#: {{ant|en|afterglow}}
#: {{ux|en|I really enjoyed yesterday’s party, but now I have the biggest '''hangover''' – I’ll not be doing that again any time soon.}}
# Similar negative effects caused by previous excessive consumption of another substance, such as a [[drug]], [[coffee]], [[sugar]], etc.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Suzanne Barnett; Jennifer Barnett Lesman; Amy Barnett Buchanan; Bev West|title=3 Fat Chicks on a Diet|publisher=St. Martin's Press|isbn=9781429901505
|passage=Don't go overboard and find yourself with a sugar '''hangover''' that lasts for days and makes your diet days that much harder.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Elizabeth Wurtzel|title=More, Now, Again|publisher=Simon and Schuster|isbn=9780743226004|page=4
|passage=So today I walk into Dr. Singer's office with a heroin '''hangover''', a headache like hell, vomiting, shaking, jonesing. I cannot bear to admit to her that it's come to this. I've been doing so well. But I missed therapy the whole time I was locked up {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Laurie Weeks|title=Zipper Mouth|publisher=The Feminist Press at CUNY|isbn=9781558617551
|passage=On the other hand, I was already drunk, and wasn't a heroin '''hangover''' preferable to the alcohol kind any day of the week?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|author=Alexandra Jamieson|title=Women, Food, and Desire|publisher=Simon and Schuster|isbn=9781476765051|page=7
|passage=We're left with our unmet needs and a sugar high that will quickly turn into a sugar '''hangover'''. So the craving rises again, calling out to us “Feed me!” and again we take the easy route and stuff it back down with food.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2018|author=Pat Fitzpatrick|title=No Sex, No Sleep|publisher=Mercier Press Ltd|isbn=9781781175330
|passage=You know nothing about despair until you have experienced a coffee '''hangover'''. This is where you lose the run of yourself and have two double espressos in a row. Ten minutes later you have a weird feeling you are going to puke out through your toes.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} An unpleasant [[relic]] left from prior events.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=7 Feb 81|volume=8|number=28|journal=Gay Community News|page=13|title=A Gentle Guy|author=Nancy Walker|text=The one '''hangover''' from high school that colored part of my college career was the social requirement of having a boyfriend.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Simon Jenkins|url=http://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2013/aug/14/gibraltar-falklands-deny-logic-history |title=Gibraltar and the Falklands deny the logic of history|work=The Guardian|date=14 August 2013|passage=While they deny the logic of history and geography, neither Gibraltar nor the Falklands will ever be truly "safe". One day these '''hangovers''' will somehow merge into their hinterlands and cease to be grit in the shoe of international relations. This day will be hastened if world governments take action to end tax havens.}}
# {{lb|en|historical}} A sleeping arrangement, usually in [[homeless shelter]]s, over a [[rope]].
#* {{RQ:Orwell Down and Out|chapter=37|passage=At the Twopenny '''Hangover''', the lodgers sit in a row on a bench; there is a rope in front of them, and they lean on this as though leaning over a fence. A man, humorously called the valet, cuts the rope at five in the morning.}}

